title: $:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/plugins/resizer
tags: [[$:/tags/Stylesheet]]
code-body: yes

\rules only filteredtranscludeinline transcludeinline macrodef macrocallinline html

<$list filter="[[$:/plugins/BTC/resizer]is[tiddler]]" variable="ignore">

/* ==========================================================================
** Resizer — placing the grip
**
** $:/plugins/BTC/resizer turns a drag into a number. It does not know where
** the thing being dragged is, and in this family it cannot: the sidebar's
** position comes out of layout.tid's variables, which are resolved from the
** active theme at render time.
**
** So the work is split three ways, the same way it is for the minimap:
**
**   the plugin   the widget, the drag, the constraint arithmetic, the
**                double-click reset.
**   this sheet   where the grip sits, in all four combinations of layout
**                mode and sidebar side, and how it behaves as the sidebar
**                slides.
**   each theme   what the grip is made of.
**
** The invocation itself is in core's sidebar.tid, which explains what each
** layout mode drags and why.
**
** One thing this sheet deliberately does NOT do is fight the plugin over the
** story river. The plugin ships a block of Vanilla-shaped geometry — an
** !important margin-right on the river among it — which overrides the margin
** the layout animates and freezes the river in place while the sidebar slides.
** Overriding that from here would have meant restating the entire layout matrix
** (two modes x two sides x full-width, plus the minimap's contribution to each)
** at !important, in a second place, where it would drift out of step with the
** layout sheet the first time either changed.
**
** Instead every theme here carries `manages-sidebar-layout: yes` in its
** plugin.info, and the plugin skips that block while such a theme is active.
** A field rather than a config tiddler, because the damage is invisible at
** rest — the resting margin is identical either way, and only the transition
** breaks — so anything switchable would eventually get switched.
** ========================================================================== */

/*
** The grip's thickness. A token because two things need it — the strip itself,
** and the negative margin that centres the strip ON the boundary rather than
** beside it — and because a theme may want a different weight of line.
**
** The coarse-pointer widening is the plugin's own convention (it does the same
** for the editor handle); a 6px target is fine for a mouse and not for a
** finger.
*/
html {
	--btc-resizer-w: 6px;
}

@media (pointer: coarse) {
	html {
		--btc-resizer-w: 14px;
	}
}

/*
** Below the breakpoint there is no sidebar to resize — it becomes a full-width
** panel — so the grip goes away entirely.
**
** The plugin hides it too, but at $:/themes/tiddlywiki/vanilla's breakpoint,
** which core's compat shim pins at 960px whatever the active theme says. These
** two rules restate the boundary at the theme's own breakpoint, in both
** directions, so a theme that moves it takes the grip with it.
*/
.tc-sidebar-resizer {
	display: none;
}

@media (min-width: {{{ [{$:/theme}addsuffix[/metrics/sidebarbreakpoint]get[text]] }}}) {

	.btc-sidebar-open .tc-sidebar-resizer {
		display: block;
	}

	/*
	** The grip is fixed to the VIEWPORT, not to the sidebar.
	**
	** It is rendered as a sibling of .tc-sidebar-scrollable rather than inside
	** it, and this is what makes that necessary: the sidebar carries
	** `translate` and `will-change: translate` to do the slide, which makes it
	** the containing block for any fixed-position descendant, and it clips its
	** own overflow. A grip on its leading edge, positioned from inside, would
	** be measured against the wrong box and then clipped away. Fixed to the
	** viewport it is measured against the viewport, which is the frame every
	** number below is already expressed in.
	**
	** `inset-inline: auto` is not redundant. The plugin positions the grip with
	** a physical `left`, computed for a wrapper element this family does not
	** render; clearing both inline insets here means each case below has to
	** state only the one edge it actually anchors to, and means the physical
	** value cannot survive into a right-to-left wiki as an offset from the
	** wrong side.
	**
	** --btc-menubar-h and not --btc-story-top: the grip spans the sidebar, and
	** the sidebar clears the menubar but not the river's own top offset. Same
	** reasoning as layout.tid's rule for .tc-sidebar-scrollable.
	*/
	.btc-sidebar-open .tc-sidebar-resizer {
		position: fixed;
		inset-block: var(--btc-menubar-h) 0;
		inset-inline: auto;
		inline-size: var(--btc-resizer-w);
		cursor: ew-resize;

		/*
		** Above the sidebar, below everything that is meant to float over the
		** page. The sidebar takes no z-index of its own and comes later in the
		** document, so without one here it would paint over the grip; dropdowns
		** are at 1000 and the top bar at 1200, and the grip must stay under
		** both.
		*/
		z-index: 500;
	}

	/*
	** Centred on the boundary.
	**
	** Each case below anchors the grip to the edge the sidebar meets the river
	** at, which puts the strip entirely on one side of it. Pulling it back by
	** half its width on the same side it is anchored from straddles the line
	** instead — so the cursor change happens where the eye expects it, and
	** widening the grip grows it symmetrically rather than pushing it off the
	** seam.
	**
	** WHICH side is the anchored one is not the same as which side the sidebar
	** is on, and conflating the two is how this was wrong the first time. Three
	** of the four cases below measure from the inline START — fixed-fluid does
	** it on both sides, because in that mode the seam is the river's outer edge
	** and the river is measured from the start whichever edge the sidebar took.
	** Only fluid-fixed with the sidebar on the end measures from the end. So the
	** start pull is the default and that one case overrides it.
	*/
	.btc-sidebar-open .tc-sidebar-resizer {
		margin-inline-start: calc(var(--btc-resizer-w) / -2);
	}

	.btc-sidebar-open.btc-layout-fluid-fixed:not(.btc-sidebar-start) .tc-sidebar-resizer {
		margin-inline-start: 0;
		margin-inline-end: calc(var(--btc-resizer-w) / -2);
	}

	/* ---- fluid-fixed ------------------------------------------------------
	**
	** The sidebar is a fixed --btc-sidebar-w column pinned to one edge, so the
	** boundary is exactly that far in — and it MOVES as the sidebar leaves.
	** Scaling by --btc-open is what makes the grip travel with it on the same
	** single transition rather than disappearing from one place and reappearing
	** in another. It is hidden by the time --btc-open reaches 0, but it is
	** visible for the whole journey.
	**
	** The minimap's strip is added on the end side and not on the start side,
	** because that is where the map is: core's plugin-minimap.tid moves the
	** sidebar in by the same amount, and the grip has to land on the sidebar's
	** new edge rather than its old one.
	*/
	.btc-sidebar-open.btc-layout-fluid-fixed:not(.btc-sidebar-start) .tc-sidebar-resizer {
		inset-inline-end: calc(
			var(--btc-open) * var(--btc-sidebar-w) + var(--btc-minimap-reserve, 0px)
		);
	}

	.btc-sidebar-open.btc-layout-fluid-fixed.btc-sidebar-start .tc-sidebar-resizer {
		inset-inline-start: calc(var(--btc-open) * var(--btc-sidebar-w));
	}

	/* ---- fixed-fluid ------------------------------------------------------
	**
	** Here the RIVER holds the fixed width and the sidebar takes what is left,
	** so the boundary is the river's outer edge — --btc-story-right — and it
	** does not move when the sidebar goes. The sidebar slides out from under
	** it by transform; the seam itself stays where it is. Hence no --btc-open
	** and no minimap term: the map changes what the sidebar spans, not where
	** the river ends.
	**
	** On the start side the sidebar's own width is stated as
	** `max(0px, 100vw - --btc-story-right)` (layout.tid explains why it is a
	** width rather than a pair of insets), so the boundary is that same
	** expression measured from the start. Restating it rather than deriving it
	** keeps the two literally identical.
	*/
	.btc-sidebar-open.btc-layout-fixed-fluid:not(.btc-sidebar-start) .tc-sidebar-resizer {
		inset-inline-start: var(--btc-story-right);
	}

	.btc-sidebar-open.btc-layout-fixed-fluid.btc-sidebar-start .tc-sidebar-resizer {
		inset-inline-start: max(0px, calc(100vw - var(--btc-story-right)));
	}
}

/*
** During a drag.
**
** The plugin drops a transparent full-viewport overlay to keep pointer capture,
** and puts `tc-resizing` on the body. Two things follow that this family cares
** about.
**
** content-visibility: auto on story frames (layout.tid, "Cost control") makes
** the browser skip offscreen tiddlers — but in fluid-fixed every frame in the
** river genuinely reflows on every pointer move, and re-measuring skipped
** frames as they cross the viewport edge is what makes a drag feel like it is
** catching. Pinning the intrinsic size for the duration keeps the scroll height
** stable while the widths change.
**
** The second is the hover surface: whatever a theme does on :hover would
** otherwise flicker off the moment the pointer leaves the six pixels, which is
** immediately. .tc-resizer-active is the plugin's own class for "being
** dragged", and every theme sheet styles it alongside :hover for that reason.
*/
body.tc-resizing .tc-story-river .tc-tiddler-frame {
	content-visibility: auto;
	contain-intrinsic-size: auto 480px;
}

/*
** RTL is left alone on purpose, and unlike the minimap it is nearly free here:
** every rule above is written in logical properties, so the grip lands on the
** correct edge in a right-to-left wiki by itself. What is NOT handled is the
** drag direction — `invert` is computed in sidebar.tid from which side the
** sidebar is on, not from the text direction, so in RTL the pointer and the
** panel disagree. Fixing that belongs with the invocation, not here.
*/

/* ---- The editor's preview handle --------------------------------------- */

/*
** This one the plugin does render itself, from its override of
** $:/core/ui/EditTemplate/body/default, and it places it too: the template
** writes a three-column grid-template inline, and the handle takes the middle
** track through `grid-area: resizer`.
**
** Inline styles beat a stylesheet, so that template silently replaces the
** two-column editor grid each theme sets up in its base.tid. That is the
** correct outcome and worth stating explicitly, because it is not obvious from
** either file alone — the theme's grid rules look like they still apply and
** they do not.
**
** All that is left is to stop it being taller than the tracks beside it. The
** plugin gives it a small asymmetric block margin tuned to Vanilla's editor
** padding; this family's editors sit flush, so the handle stretches instead.
*/
.tc-tiddler-frame .tc-tiddler-editor.tc-tiddler-preview .tc-tiddler-editor-resizer {
	margin-block: 0;
	align-self: stretch;
}

/* ==========================================================================
** Resizer — Ironworks
**
** Where the grip goes is not here — it is placed once, above, and this half is
** only about what it is made of. The two live in one file because they answer
** to one plugin; the banner is the seam between them.
**
** In this theme the seam between the river and the sidebar is the joint
** between two plates, and it is treated exactly like every other cut edge in
** the shop: a dark line where the metal ends and a catch of light on the side
** the light comes from. Nothing else. There is no knurling, no lever, no
** machined thumbwheel — a handle would be a prop, and this theme reads
** materials rather than dressing up as one.
**
** What DOES change is the temperature. At rest the joint is cold stock. Under
** the hand it runs hot, because heat is what this theme already means by
** "live": the ember accent is the primary key, the focus catch and the drag
** target everywhere else in it.
** ========================================================================== */

/*
** The bar carries nothing.
**
** The plugin fills it with muted-foreground at 0.75 — a flat grey band the
** height of the page, the only unworked surface in the theme. The bar stays as
** the hit target; the joint itself is drawn one pixel wide by the
** pseudo-element below.
*/
.tc-sidebar-resizer,
.tc-tiddler-editor-resizer {
	background-color: transparent;
	opacity: 1;
}

/*
** The joint.
**
** --iw-plate-edge for the cut and --iw-lit for the catch beside it is the pair
** this theme uses on every edge that is meant to read as thickness; using them
** here is what makes the seam a piece of the same material rather than a
** control that arrived from somewhere else.
**
** The catch is offset by calc(--btc-dir * 1px) rather than a bare 1px because
** box-shadow offsets are physical. --btc-dir is +1 in a left-to-right wiki and
** -1 in a right-to-left one, so the light stays on the same side of the cut as
** it does on every other edge in the theme when the page flips.
**
** Radius zero, stated rather than assumed: the plugin's default is a rounded
** pill, and a radius is a moulding artefact. Nothing here is moulded.
*/
.tc-sidebar-resizer::before,
.tc-tiddler-editor-resizer::before {
	inline-size: 1px;
	block-size: 100%;
	border-radius: 0;
	background: var(--iw-plate-edge);
	box-shadow: calc(var(--btc-dir) * 1px) 0 0 var(--iw-lit);
	transition:
		background-color var(--btc-dur) var(--btc-ease),
		box-shadow var(--btc-dur) var(--btc-ease),
		inline-size var(--btc-dur) var(--btc-ease);
}

/*
** Under the hand it runs hot.
**
** --iw-oxide is the ember worked into the foreground — the same colour the
** theme uses for a live control rather than for decoration. The light catch is
** dropped and replaced by a short bleed of the same colour on both sides: a
** cut edge catches light, a hot one gives it off, and keeping the catch as
** well would be lighting the joint from two contradictory directions.
**
** .tc-resizer-active is the plugin's class for "being dragged", styled with
** :hover rather than after it — once the drag starts the pointer is nowhere
** near the six pixels it began on, and a hover-only rule would go cold at
** exactly the moment the thing is being used.
*/
.tc-sidebar-resizer:hover::before,
.tc-sidebar-resizer.tc-resizer-active::before,
.tc-tiddler-editor-resizer:hover::before,
.tc-tiddler-editor-resizer.tc-resizer-active::before {
	inline-size: 2px;
	background: var(--iw-oxide);
	box-shadow: 0 0 6px 0 var(--iw-oxide-stain);
}

/*
** The plugin inverts the bar on hover — bar to primary, pill to
** muted-foreground. Both halves are restated above; this stops the bar half
** coming back and putting a solid ember band behind the joint, which would
** read as a glowing six-pixel gap rather than a hot seam.
*/
.tc-sidebar-resizer:hover,
.tc-sidebar-resizer.tc-resizer-active,
.tc-tiddler-editor-resizer:hover,
.tc-tiddler-editor-resizer.tc-resizer-active {
	background-color: transparent;
	opacity: 1;
}

/*
** The land.
**
** The cut line says where the plates meet. It does not say you can move them,
** and six pixels of hit area with a one-pixel mark on it gives the eye nothing
** to aim for.
**
** A joint that is meant to be worked gets a raised land at the point of
** purchase — thicker stock, machined proud, so a hand or a tool has something
** to bear on. That is what this is: the same cut edge, three pixels wide for
** the height of a grip, carrying the same lit-above and shade-below pair every
** raised face in this theme carries. It is a machining feature rather than a
** handle, which is the whole difference between this theme and a costume.
*/
.tc-sidebar-resizer::after,
.tc-tiddler-editor-resizer::after {
	content: "";
	position: absolute;
	inset-block-start: 50%;
	inset-inline-start: 50%;
	translate: -50% -50%;
	inline-size: 3px;
	block-size: 44px;
	background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--tpc-foreground) 26%, var(--tpc-page-background));
	box-shadow:
		inset 0 1px 0 var(--iw-lit),
		inset 0 -1px 0 var(--iw-shade);
	transition: background-color var(--btc-dur) var(--btc-ease);
}

.tc-sidebar-resizer:hover::after,
.tc-sidebar-resizer.tc-resizer-active::after,
.tc-tiddler-editor-resizer:hover::after,
.tc-tiddler-editor-resizer.tc-resizer-active::after {
	background: var(--iw-oxide);
}

/* ---- The plates, while the seam is being moved ------------------------- */

/*
** The rust comes off the plates for the duration of a sidebar drag, and the
** grit stays on.
**
** Not a taste decision. Dragging the seam changes every plate's width on every
** pointer move, and a background tile LARGER than the box it repeats into
** cannot be cached and blitted — it is regenerated at the element's geometry
** each time that geometry moves. The three stain tiles (940x780, 1180x1020,
** 700x1340) are all bigger than a plate in at least one axis, and three of them
** stacked measured ~52ms per frame across a river of eighteen plates: about
** 19fps, against a locked 60 with them off. The two grit tiles are 260 and 720,
** fit inside a plate, and cost nothing measurable — so they are what stays.
** 3-surface.tid's token block splits the patina in two for exactly this.
**
** Scoped through :has() on the SIDEBAR grip specifically, rather than through
** the `tc-resizing` class the plugin puts on the body. The body class is shared
** with the editor's preview handle, and that drag resizes the two columns
** inside one editor — no plate changes width, so there is nothing to buy and a
** river full of plates would shed its rust for no reason.
**
** .iw-patina is repeated on the page container rather than assumed, because
** this rule has to lose to nothing and win over nothing: with the patina switch
** off there is no background on a plate at all, and a rule that named only the
** drag would put the grit back on a theme the user had asked to keep clean.
*/
.iw-patina.tc-page-container:has(.tc-sidebar-resizer.tc-resizer-active) .tc-tiddler-frame {
	background-image: var(--iw-patina-grit);
	background-size: var(--iw-patina-grit-size);
}

</$list>
